('127.696.d.5.A',4,'Identifying essential tasks first lets designers:','["remove all buttons", "focus the interface on what users must accomplish", "hide the program", "add random features"]'::jsonb,1,'Task focus drives design.','original'),
('127.696.d.5.B',3,'A user types letters into a field meant for a number. The program should handle this:','["color blindness", "power failure", "unexpected input", "hardware wear"]'::jsonb,2,'Programs must handle bad input.','original'),
('127.696.d.5.B',4,'A developer predicts that new users might click ''Submit'' with an empty form. This is identifying:','["a finished feature", "a licensing term", "a battery issue", "a point of potential confusion"]'::jsonb,3,'Anticipating confusion improves design.','original'),
('127.696.d.5.C',3,'A clear interface uses labeled buttons that:','["tell the user what each button does", "are all the same color without labels", "change names randomly", "hide their purpose"]'::jsonb,0,'Clarity guides users.','original'),
('127.696.d.5.C',4,'A well-designed interface reduces errors by:','["using tiny fonts", "guiding the user with clear prompts and feedback", "removing all text", "adding secret menus"]'::jsonb,1,'Prompts and feedback prevent mistakes.','original'),
('127.696.d.5.D',3,'Supplemental instructions such as a README or FAQ explain things like:','["how to paint the case", "how to rewrite the OS", "how to start the program", "how to buy a computer"]'::jsonb,2,'Supplements cover what the interface can''t.','original'),
('127.696.d.5.D',4,'A frequently asked questions page helps users by:','["replacing the interface", "hiding the program", "deleting errors", "answering common questions in one place"]'::jsonb,3,'FAQs reduce confusion.','original'),
('127.696.d.5.E',3,'Testing a program with someone who has never seen it helps find:','["confusing steps the creator no longer notices", "fan noise", "compiler flags", "syntax colors"]'::jsonb,0,'Fresh eyes expose blind spots.','original'),
('127.696.d.5.E',4,'During a new-user test, the developer should mostly:','["explain every step", "observe quietly and take notes", "do the task for the user", "argue with the tester"]'::jsonb,1,'Observing shows real behavior.','original'),
('127.696.d.5.F',3,'After new-user testing, five of six testers couldn''t find the Save button. This result suggests:','["the manual is too long", "the code is perfect", "the button needs to be easier to find", "the testers are careless"]'::jsonb,2,'Common failures point to design problems.','original'),
('127.696.d.5.F',4,'When evaluating feedback, a developer should prioritize:','["a single unusual opinion", "the cheapest fix only", "the loudest comment only", "problems that several users hit"]'::jsonb,3,'Repeated issues matter most.','original'),
('127.696.d.5.G',3,'A team moves the Save button and adds a label after testing. This is:','["refining the program based on feedback", "ignoring results", "cancelling the project", "starting over"]'::jsonb,0,'Iteration improves designs.','original'),
('127.696.d.5.G',4,'After revising a program, the instructions should also be:','["deleted", "updated to match the changes", "left unchanged", "hidden"]'::jsonb,1,'Documentation must match the product.','original'),
('127.696.d.5.H',3,'After making changes from user feedback, the team should:','["delete the feedback", "ship without testing", "re-test the program with users", "stop the project"]'::jsonb,2,'Retesting confirms the fixes work.','original'),
('127.696.d.5.H',4,'At the end of a re-test, the team identifies next steps such as:','["hiding the results", "destroying the code", "changing teams", "remaining issues to fix and features to add"]'::jsonb,3,'Next steps carry the project forward.','original'),
('127.696.d.6.A',3,'A tester enters a letter where a number is expected to see if the program crashes. This is:','["break testing", "flowcharting", "code compression", "licensing"]'::jsonb,0,'Break testing tries to make the program fail.','original'),
('127.696.d.6.A',4,'Writing tests for an existing program mainly determines whether it:','["is open source", "works as intended under many inputs", "uses short names", "has pretty colors"]'::jsonb,1,'Tests confirm functionality.','original'),
('127.696.d.6.B',3,'While reading old code, a student finds a function that does something not mentioned in its comments. This is:','["a planned feature", "a licensing step", "an unexpected finding", "a flowchart symbol"]'::jsonb,2,'Deconstruction can reveal surprises.','original'),
('127.696.d.6.B',4,'When deconstructing code gives unexpected results, the best response is to:','["assume it is fine", "delete the code", "ignore it", "describe what was found and investigate why"]'::jsonb,3,'Unexpected behavior needs explanation.','original'),
('127.696.d.6.C',3,'Before using a software library, a developer should read its documentation to learn its:','["uses and functionality", "author''s hobbies", "price tag", "logo"]'::jsonb,0,'Docs explain what a library can do.','original'),
('127.696.d.6.C',4,'A programmer imports a library for math functions instead of writing them. This choice mainly:','["hides bugs forever", "saves time by reusing tested code", "removes the need for testing", "makes the code unreadable"]'::jsonb,1,'Libraries provide reusable functionality.','original'),
('127.696.d.6.D',3,'Constructing a flowchart for an existing program shows:','["the programmer''s age", "the mouse type", "the program''s logic and flow", "the monitor size"]'::jsonb,2,'Flowcharts visualize program flow.','original'),
('127.696.d.6.D',4,'To build a flowchart from existing code, a student traces the code and draws:','["only the last line", "random shapes", "the file names only", "each step and decision in order"]'::jsonb,3,'Trace the logic in order.','original'),
('127.696.d.6.E',3,'A customer needs a quiz that tracks scores, but the program only asks questions. Comparing these shows:','["a gap between functionality and needs", "a perfect match", "a new logo", "a faster computer"]'::jsonb,0,'Comparison reveals missing features.','original'),
('127.696.d.6.E',4,'A fair comparison of a program to customer needs uses:','["the developer''s opinion only", "a list of the customer''s stated requirements", "the number of lines", "the program''s file size"]'::jsonb,1,'Requirements are the yardstick.','original'),
('127.696.d.6.F',3,'A flowchart lacks a step to save results, which the customer required. The team should:','["delete the requirement", "remove the flowchart", "add the missing step to the flowchart", "ignore the customer"]'::jsonb,2,'Update designs to include requirements.','original'),
('127.696.d.6.F',4,'Adding missing specifications to a flowchart before coding helps by:','["wasting time", "hiding bugs", "deleting data", "planning the change before writing code"]'::jsonb,3,'Planning avoids rework.','original'),
('127.696.d.6.G',3,'A developer adds a score-saving feature to a quiz program. This is:','["developing new code to meet a specification", "copying a competitor", "removing a feature", "breaking the program"]'::jsonb,0,'New code adds required features.','original'),
('127.696.d.6.G',4,'After writing new code, the developer should explain it by:','["using single-letter names", "using comments and documentation", "hiding the code", "deleting the old notes"]'::jsonb,1,'Explanation aids future readers.','original'),
('127.696.d.6.H',3,'The team predicted the app would load in 1 second; it took 5. Comparing these helps to:','["skip testing", "hide the result", "find where the product needs redesign", "prove the design is perfect"]'::jsonb,2,'Gaps between prediction and result guide redesign.','original'),
('127.696.d.6.H',4,'A gap between predicted and actual functionality is best treated as:','["proof of success", "a reason to give up", "something to hide", "useful data for generating redesign ideas"]'::jsonb,3,'Differences point to improvements.','original'),
('127.696.d.7.A',3,'A black box model shows a system''s:','["inputs and outputs without its inner workings", "wiring colors", "every line of code", "team members"]'::jsonb,0,'Black boxes hide internals.','original'),
('127.696.d.7.A',4,'A functional model differs from a black box model because it shows:','["only the cost", "the internal functions and how they connect", "only the final product", "no inputs"]'::jsonb,1,'Functional models open the box.','original'),
('127.696.d.7.B',3,'In a gallery walk, team members:','["grade tests", "buy parts", "view each other''s ideas and give feedback", "write final code"]'::jsonb,2,'Gallery walks share and critique ideas.','original'),
('127.696.d.7.B',4,'The main rule of brainstorming''s idea-generation stage is to:','["work alone in silence", "reject silly ideas", "pick one idea immediately", "produce many ideas without criticizing them yet"]'::jsonb,3,'Quantity first, judgment later.','original'),
('127.696.d.7.C',3,'A Pugh chart compares design concepts by rating them against:','["a baseline using the same criteria", "the budget of rivals", "the date", "the team''s mood"]'::jsonb,0,'Pugh charts compare to a baseline.','original'),
('127.696.d.7.C',4,'A weighted decision matrix gives more influence to criteria that are:','["listed first", "more important to the project", "less costly", "easier to measure only"]'::jsonb,1,'Weights reflect importance.','original'),
('127.696.d.8.A',3,'A student chooses a ruler for measuring and a multimeter for checking voltage. This is:','["breaking safety rules", "ignoring specs", "selecting appropriate tools for the task", "skipping design"]'::jsonb,2,'Match tools to tasks.','original'),
('127.696.d.8.A',4,'Using the wrong tool for a task can:','["protect the device", "save all time", "improve accuracy", "cause errors or injury"]'::jsonb,3,'Appropriate tools prevent problems.','original'),
('127.696.d.8.B',3,'An engineering notebook is used to:','["record design decisions, tests and results", "play games", "print posters", "store music"]'::jsonb,0,'Notebooks document the process.','original'),
('127.696.d.8.B',4,'A good notebook entry includes the date, what was done and:','["unrelated doodles", "the results and next steps", "the weather only", "the cafeteria menu"]'::jsonb,1,'Complete entries support later review.','original'),
('127.696.d.8.C',3,'A developer writes a small piece of code, tests it, and fixes errors before adding more. This is:','["avoiding all testing", "waiting until the end to test", "developing, testing and refining throughout the process", "copying code"]'::jsonb,2,'Iterative testing catches errors early.','original'),
('127.696.d.8.C',4,'Testing small code sections often is useful because it:','["hides bugs", "slows all progress forever", "removes the need for design", "makes bugs easier to locate"]'::jsonb,3,'Smaller scope narrows the search.','original'),
('127.696.d.8.D',3,'A schematic uses symbols to show:','["how circuit components are connected", "the text of the code", "the programmer''s age", "the grading scale"]'::jsonb,0,'Schematics are electrical diagrams.','original'),
('127.696.d.8.D',4,'When building a circuit from a diagram, a student should check that each connection:','["is random", "matches the diagram before powering on", "is hidden", "is guessed"]'::jsonb,1,'Verify before applying power.','original'),
('127.696.d.8.E',3,'A solderless breadboard lets students:','["charge phones", "print code", "connect components without soldering", "store files"]'::jsonb,2,'Breadboards allow quick prototyping.','original'),
('127.696.d.8.E',4,'A microcontroller circuit with an LED usually needs a resistor to:','["make it brighter forever", "change its color", "speed up the code", "limit the current so the LED isn''t damaged"]'::jsonb,3,'Resistors limit current.','original'),
('127.696.d.8.F',3,'Starting a design from an existing baseline design means:','["improving something that already exists", "avoiding testing", "ignoring all prior work", "starting with no ideas"]'::jsonb,0,'Baselines give a starting point.','original'),
('127.696.d.8.F',4,'Beginning with a baseline design is useful because it:','["removes the need for testing", "gives a reference to compare and improve", "guarantees success", "eliminates constraints"]'::jsonb,1,'Baselines enable comparison.','original'),
('127.696.d.8.G',3,'Running a simulation of a robot arm before building it helps to:','["skip planning", "damage real parts", "test hardware ideas without risk or cost", "avoid all design"]'::jsonb,2,'Simulations mimic real events.','original'),
('127.696.d.8.G',4,'A simulation is useful for hardware development because it:','["is always perfect", "replaces all testing forever", "needs no inputs", "predicts behavior before parts are built"]'::jsonb,3,'Models predict outcomes.','original'),
('127.696.d.8.H',3,'A limitation of a simulation is that it:','["simplifies real-world conditions", "has no assumptions", "is always exact", "never needs data"]'::jsonb,0,'Models leave out details.','original'),
('127.696.d.8.H',4,'When critiquing a model, a team should explain both its usefulness and its:','["speed only", "limitations", "logo", "price"]'::jsonb,1,'Good critiques cover both sides.','original'),
('127.696.d.8.I',3,'A team builds a working version, tests it against the requirements and improves it. This is the:','["shipping process", "licensing step", "prototype cycle", "marketing plan"]'::jsonb,2,'Prototypes are built, tested and refined.','original'),
('127.696.d.8.I',4,'A prototype fails a requirement during testing. The team should:','["delete the requirement quietly", "ignore the failure", "ship it anyway", "refine the prototype and test again"]'::jsonb,3,'Fix and retest.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
join public.teks_items i on i.code = v.code and i.is_standard
join public.teks_documents d on d.id = i.document_id and d.subject = 'Career Development' and d.language = 'en'
on conflict (fingerprint) do nothing;
insert into public.question_bank (teks_code, teks_item_id, subject, grade_num, course, difficulty, qtype, prompt, choices, answer_idx, explanation, source, status, gen_batch)
select distinct on (v.prompt) v.code, i.id, d.subject,
       case when i.grade_level = 'K' then 0 when i.grade_level ~ '^[0-9]+' then substring(i.grade_level from '^[0-9]+')::smallint end,
       case when i.grade_level is null then i.grade_course_title end,
       v.diff, 'mc', v.prompt, v.choices, v.ans, v.expl, v.src, 'checked', 'cte_89_v1'
from (values
('127.696.d.8.J',3,'After prototyping, the team reports the product''s final design by describing its:','["features, specifications and results", "stray ideas", "unused parts", "lunch plans"]'::jsonb,0,'Final reports summarize the design.','original'),
('127.696.d.8.J',4,'A final design report should explain:','["only the cost of tape", "how the design meets requirements", "the team''s favorite songs", "the weather"]'::jsonb,1,'Reports show requirements were met.','original'),
('127.696.d.9.A',3,'A robot arm''s joint angle can be found from side lengths using:','["word processing", "a spreadsheet''s color", "trigonometry", "file compression"]'::jsonb,2,'Trig relates angles and sides.','original'),
('127.696.d.9.A',4,'Given two segments of an arm and a target point, the law of cosines helps to:','["compress an image", "rename the variables", "draw a flowchart", "calculate the joint angle needed"]'::jsonb,3,'Geometry gives angles from lengths.','original'),
('127.696.d.9.B',3,'A program that repeats a calculation each time a sensor updates uses a:','["loop", "header", "comment", "license"]'::jsonb,0,'Loops repeat calculations.','original'),
('127.696.d.9.B',4,'Applying math cyclically in a program means the algorithm:','["only prints text", "runs the same calculation repeatedly on new data", "never uses numbers", "runs once and stops"]'::jsonb,1,'Cyclic algorithms repeat.','original'),
('127.696.d.9.C',3,'Checking whether an algorithm gives correct results for many test cases is:','["naming variables", "buying parts", "verifying the algorithm", "painting the case"]'::jsonb,2,'Verification confirms correctness.','original'),
('127.696.d.9.C',4,'Between two correct algorithms, the more efficient one:','["has more lines", "costs more", "is always longer", "uses fewer steps or resources"]'::jsonb,3,'Efficiency means fewer resources.','original'),
('127.696.d.10.A',3,'Software that reads a button press and turns on a motor is a:','["software interface that communicates with hardware", "word document", "spreadsheet", "paper map"]'::jsonb,0,'It bridges software and hardware.','original'),
('127.696.d.10.A',4,'A microcontroller program controls a servo by sending:','["a letter", "a signal to the pin the servo is wired to", "a printed page", "an email"]'::jsonb,1,'Hardware control is through pins.','original'),
('127.696.d.10.B',3,'A robot design uses a function to compute speed and needs to know how motors use electricity. This applies ideas from:','["music only", "art only", "computer science and science together", "history only"]'::jsonb,2,'Engineering combines subjects.','original'),
('127.696.d.10.B',4,'Understanding mechanics helps a programmer who controls a robot arm because it explains:','["how emails are sent", "how web pages load", "how fonts are drawn", "how forces and motion affect movement"]'::jsonb,3,'Mechanics governs motion.','original'),
('127.696.d.10.C',3,'A program converts a raw sensor reading of 0–1023 into a distance in centimeters. This is:','["abstraction using intuitive variables", "a comment", "a hardware repair", "a license"]'::jsonb,0,'Abstraction hides raw numbers.','original'),
('127.696.d.10.C',4,'Naming a function getDistanceCm() instead of using raw values everywhere makes code:','["harder to read", "easier to read and use", "unable to be tested", "longer to run"]'::jsonb,1,'Abstraction makes code intuitive.','original'),
('127.696.d.11.A',3,'A team combines a motor-control program and a sensor program into one robot program. This is:','["a licensing step", "drawing a flowchart", "integrating small programs into a larger solution", "deleting code"]'::jsonb,2,'Systems thinking joins parts.','original'),
('127.696.d.11.A',4,'When integrating small programs, the team should check that the parts:','["are all deleted", "have identical names", "are all hidden", "work correctly together"]'::jsonb,3,'Integration testing confirms cooperation.','original'),
('127.696.d.11.B',3,'A variable named speedLimit is better than x because it:','["tells readers what the value means", "is faster", "uses less memory", "is shorter"]'::jsonb,0,'Intuitive names aid readability.','original'),
('127.696.d.11.B',4,'Adding comments to a block of code mainly helps:','["the code run backward", "other people and future you understand it", "the computer store less data", "the compiler run faster"]'::jsonb,1,'Comments aid readers.','original'),
('127.696.d.11.C',3,'In a program, a picture can be stored as a:','["list of fonts", "single letter", "grid (array) of pixel values", "spreadsheet formula"]'::jsonb,2,'Images are arrays of numbers.','original'),
('127.696.d.11.C',4,'Storing the frequency 440 in a variable named noteA is an example of:','["encryption", "deletion", "compression", "abstraction"]'::jsonb,3,'Names stand for numbers.','original'),
('127.696.d.11.D',3,'A library function needs a particular data type, but the image is in another. The programmer must:','["convert the image data to the required type", "delete the image", "ignore the error", "rename the function"]'::jsonb,0,'Types must match.','original'),
('127.696.d.11.D',4,'An error says an image array has the wrong type for a function. The best fix is to:','["rename the file", "convert the array to the type the function expects", "delete the function", "unplug the computer"]'::jsonb,1,'Convert data to match library requirements.','original'),
('127.696.d.11.E',3,'A program that updates an image whenever a user moves a slider uses:','["a single print statement", "a static image only", "a loop and conditions to respond to input in real time", "no logic"]'::jsonb,2,'Loops and ifs react to inputs.','original'),
('127.696.d.11.E',4,'A trackbar value changes a brightness threshold. The program shows the new image by:','["ignoring the trackbar", "running once", "hiding the image", "re-applying the threshold each loop and redrawing"]'::jsonb,3,'Repeated updates show changes live.','original'),
('127.696.d.11.F',3,'Writing steps in plain English that outline a program''s logic before coding is:','["pseudocode", "a comment-only file", "a compiler", "machine code"]'::jsonb,0,'Pseudocode plans logic in words.','original'),
('127.696.d.11.F',4,'Together, flowcharts, pseudocode and comments serve to:','["slow down coding", "document and explain software design", "hide design decisions", "replace testing"]'::jsonb,1,'They explain the design.','original'),
('127.696.d.11.G',3,'An interface that shows sensor values to a user and also controls a motor communicates with:','["only the compiler", "nothing", "both users and hardware", "only paper"]'::jsonb,2,'Interfaces bridge users and devices.','original'),
('127.696.d.11.G',4,'A good interface for a robot controller shows the user the:','["internet history", "programmer''s name", "code''s file size", "current status and available controls"]'::jsonb,3,'Clear status and controls aid users.','original'),
('127.696.d.11.H',3,'Using a library''s function without knowing how it works inside is treating it as a:','["black box", "legal notice", "glass box", "blank page"]'::jsonb,0,'Black box use relies on inputs and outputs.','original'),
('127.696.d.11.H',4,'Treating imported code as a black box means a programmer only needs to know its:','["author''s address", "inputs and what it returns", "commit history", "internal line count"]'::jsonb,1,'Interface, not internals.','original'),
('127.696.d.11.I',3,'Representing a robot''s joint as four points in a plane is an example of:','["compression", "encryption", "abstraction", "cabling"]'::jsonb,2,'Simple points stand for a complex joint.','original'),
('127.696.d.11.I',4,'Four points describing a joint help a program calculate:','["the battery type", "the code color", "the file size", "the angle between the connected segments"]'::jsonb,3,'Points give geometry.','original'),
('127.696.d.11.J',3,'A student says "I wrote a function to repeat these steps." This shows correct use of:','["programming vocabulary", "safety signs", "wiring symbols", "engineering notebooks"]'::jsonb,0,'Right terms communicate clearly.','original'),
('127.696.d.11.J',4,'Using terms like variable, loop and function accurately helps a team to:','["hide their work", "communicate clearly about code", "avoid testing", "shorten programs"]'::jsonb,1,'Shared vocabulary supports collaboration.','original'),
('127.696.d.12.A',3,'Video is processed as a sequence of:','["spreadsheet cells", "single words", "image frames", "audio-only files"]'::jsonb,2,'Videos are frame sequences.','original'),
('127.696.d.12.A',4,'Organizing image data into arrays and frames before processing makes it:','["unreadable", "impossible to analyze", "unchangeable", "possible to apply algorithms in a consistent way"]'::jsonb,3,'Structure enables processing.','original'),
('127.696.d.12.B',3,'A program analyzes camera data and tells the user a part is misaligned. It used data to:','["make decisions and instruct users", "print posters", "buy parts", "recolor the case"]'::jsonb,0,'Analysis supports decisions.','original'),
('127.696.d.12.B',4,'Analyzing complex data to instruct users requires the program to turn raw numbers into:','["random sounds", "clear, meaningful information", "more raw numbers", "blank screens"]'::jsonb,1,'Clear output helps users act.','original'),
('127.696.d.12.C',3,'A program reads sensor data, runs an algorithm and sends a motor command. It creates:','["a licensing file", "a paper copy", "output commands from incoming data", "a new password"]'::jsonb,2,'Input to algorithm to output.','original'),
('127.696.d.12.C',4,'In a data-driven program, the algorithm''s role is to:','["print only the date", "delete the input", "store only comments", "transform input data into useful output"]'::jsonb,3,'Algorithms process data.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
